2. ImagePullBackOff / ErrImagePull
Problem: Kubernetes cannot download the container image.

Check
-------
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look at:
-----------
Failed to pull image
pull access denied
repository does not exist
unauthorized

Common causes
----------------
Wrong image name
Wrong tag
Private registry authentication missing
Image doesn't exist
Registry/network problem

Fix wrong image
containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx:1.27

Instead of:
image: nginx:wrongtag

Private registry

Create an image pull secret:
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=<registry-url> \
  --docker-username=<username> \
  --docker-password=<password>


Kubernetes creates:
type: kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson
data:
  .dockerconfigjson: <Kubernetes-generated-base64-value>

So in your YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret

metadata:
  name: regcred

type: kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson
data:
  .dockerconfigjson: <BASE64_VALUE>

<BASE64_VALUE> is generated from the Docker authentication configuration.

Important
If you want to write the actual value in the YAML yourself, you can generate it with:    cat ~/.docker/config.json | base64 -w 0

Then put the output here:
data:
  .dockerconfigjson: <OUTPUT_FROM_ABOVE_COMMAND>

So:  .dockerconfigjson = Base64-encoded Docker config containing the registry credentials.





apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: myapp-deployment

spec:
  replicas: 2

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp

    spec:
      imagePullSecrets:
        - name: regcred

      containers:
        - name: myapp
          image: <registry-url>/myapp:1.0
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080


manually means 
You can put the Docker config directly, without manually Base64-encoding it:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret

metadata:
  name: regcred

type: kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson

stringData:
  .dockerconfigjson: |
    {
      "auths": {
        "https://index.docker.io/v1/": {
          "username": "siva123",
          "password": "mypassword",
          "auth": "BASE64_USERNAME_PASSWORD"
        }
      }
    }


"auth": "BASE64_USERNAME_PASSWORD"
means Base64 encoding of username:password.

For example:
Username = siva123
Password = mypassword

First combine them:  siva123:mypassword
Then Base64 encode that:  c2l2YTEyMzpteXBhc3N3b3Jk
The auth field is essentially a Base64-encoded username:password used by Docker registry authentication.

Important: Base64 is only encoding. Anyone who can decode it can get the original value, so Kubernetes Secrets 
should also be protected with proper access controls and encryption at rest.


What this means
imagePullSecrets:
  - name: regcred

means:  "When Kubernetes tries to pull this private image, use the credentials stored in the regcred Secret."

Flow:
Deployment
    ↓
Pod
    ↓
image: private-registry/myapp:1.0
    ↓
Private Registry
    ↑
regcred Secret
    ↓
Authentication
    ↓
Image downloaded ✅

Without imagePullSecrets, if the registry is private, you may get:
ErrImagePull
ImagePullBackOff

The important point is that regcred is not the username/password itself. It is the name of the Kubernetes Secret that contains the registry credentials.
