3. Service Not Reachable
Problem: Pod is running, but Service cannot reach it.

to check
------
selector mismatch
no endpoints,  wrong targetport,network policy


Think:

Client
  ↓
Service
  ↓
Selector
  ↓
Pod
  ↓
Container
Check Service     kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl describe svc <service-name> -n <namespace>

Check endpoints   kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>
------------
or:
kubectl get endpointslices -n <namespace>


Very important
If you see:  Endpoints: <none>
the Service is not finding the pods.

Check labels
------------
kubectl get pods --show-labels -n <namespace>

Service:
selector:
  app: nginx

Pod:
labels:
  app: nginx
They must match.


Check Service port
--------------
Example:

ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8080

Meaning:
Service port 80
      ↓
Pod port 8080
Make sure the application is actually listening on 8080.


Test from inside cluster
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- curl http://<service-name>:80
Verify
kubectl get endpoints <service-name>


It means this command:  kubectl get endpointslices -n <namespace>
is used to see which Pod IP addresses are connected to a Service.
Example
Suppose you have a Service:  
Service: nginx-service
and 2 Pods:
Pod 1 → 10.0.0.5
Pod 2 → 10.0.0.6

The EndpointSlice keeps track of those backend Pod addresses.
Run:  kubectl get endpointslices -n default
You might see:
NAME             ADDRESSES         PORT
nginx-service    10.0.0.5,10.0.0.6  80

So the flow is:
Client
   ↓
Service
   ↓
EndpointSlice
   ↓
Pod 10.0.0.5
Pod 10.0.0.6
Why check it?

